A||||53||btoth||FWD or RWD better?||||3||
Z||000000||btoth||09-04-2001||12:20 AM||||OK, a friend of mine got into an argument with another friend of mine about which is better as far as grip in acceleration and overall racing. RWD or FWD? (We're leaving AWD out of this.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">) And I believe they were talking front engine cars... no mid-engine layouts.<P>If at all possible... for those of you that know your physics (I forgot my forumlas)... could you prove which is better (or if they're equal) scientifically.<P>Thanks!||||reg||3
Z||000001||sqoach||09-04-2001||12:49 AM||||Seems that FWD would do better. Front engine, RWD would spin the tires easier and not hook up as well. I know for a fact that FWD are much better in the snow and ice  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"><P>------------------<BR>-Adam<BR><FONT COLOR="BLUE">sqoach@yahoo.com</FONT><BR>        <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sqoach.gif">        <BR>^^^^^^<B>new project</B>^^^^^^<BR><LI><FONT SIZE="1">84 SE (FOR SALE)*sale pending*<BR><LI>85 Coupe (parts car)($50)*sale pending*<BR><LI>86 SC (FOR SALE)</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Neal||09-04-2001||01:07 AM||||RWD all the way<P>first drive a conventional FWD and RWD car with similar power and see witch will spin the tires easier... FWD, giving RWD a traction advantage<P>FWD gives a much higher weight at the front of the car, RWD allows the car to get closer to the majical 50/50 balance.  again RWD advantage<P>RWD gives an element of controll and vehical balance in the throtle, FWD just gives torque steer.  RWD wins again.<P>Get the car into a spin, RWD you have a hope of recovering, FWD your screwed.<P>Im a big fan of RWD in ALL situations including snow and ice.  It is simply better.||||reg||14
Z||000003||Formula88||09-04-2001||01:12 AM||||Well, in a FWD, the front wheels have to steer and accelerate.  In RWD, the load is split.  Also, RWD gives you more options in driver control.  Just look at professional racing classes, any of them, and see how many FWD cars there are.  If FWD were better, you'd see a lot of it.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Pontiaddict||09-04-2001||01:40 AM||||When accelerating, the weight shifts to the back, taking the weight off the front wheels making FWD's spin and RWD's hook better. Plus they make virtually bulletproof RWD drivetrains. Most affordable FWD parts arent as strong, since you have to make them steer the front wheels also. Then there's torque steer. All in all, I would take a POS RWD car over a nicer front driver anyday.||||reg||1
Z||000005||artherd||09-04-2001||02:03 AM||||You're kidding, right?<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
Z||000006||Pontiaddict||09-04-2001||02:54 AM||||I said nicer, not mint.||||reg||1
Z||000007||JohnnyK||09-04-2001||03:24 AM||||I guess it depends.. Sure, most people will go "RWD all the way!" because they want to seem hardcore (it's cooler, you know).  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> BUT, i KNOW that in snow and ice, FWD is a HELL of a lot better. I also believe that having the weight over the drive wheels (FWD) makes for better hook up (I don't think that there is enough weight being thrown backwards to compensate for the lack of weight over the rear wheels).  However, since racing is traditionally rwd, fwd would be blashphemy. Also, there is torque steer, yadayadayada... I'd say different advantages for different situations.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Pontiaddict||09-04-2001||03:49 AM||||I honestly havent met a FWD car that I truley liked. If that makes me hardcore, then cool, I guess. But it was not my intention. <P>I still don't agree that FWD is better in the snow and ice. And I still don't like the car trying to pull the steering wheel out of my hands when accellerating.<P>There are people who will say things just to be different/be in the majority. Or they could have said it because that's what they truly believe.<p>[This message has been edited by Pontiaddict (edited 09-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||MrPBody||09-04-2001||07:44 AM||||Mid-engine RWD, like Fiero and all successful purpose-built racers.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||Formula88||09-04-2001||09:30 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by JohnnyK:<BR><B> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> BUT, i KNOW that in snow and ice, FWD is a HELL of a lot better. I also believe that having the weight over the drive wheels (FWD) makes for better hook up (I don't think that there is enough weight being thrown backwards to compensate for the lack of weight over the rear wheels).  However, since racing is traditionally rwd, fwd would be blashphemy. Also, there is torque steer, yadayadayada... I'd say different advantages for different situations.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Well, since this thread was about racing, if you were racing in ice or snow, I'm sure FWD might help.<P>As for the weight transfer, it's easily documented.  If a car has enough power to pull a wheelstand, all of the weight is on the back tires, right?  So the more power you have, the more weight is getting transferred to the rear.  <P>Again, if FWD were better, it would be used to win races.  Racing isn't about tradition, it's about money.  The winners get the money.||||reg||6
Z||000011||Formula||09-04-2001||09:41 AM||||lower powered cars..... fwd is better (neon, cavalier, etc..)<P>hig power cars...... rwd is better (mustang, camaro, etc...)<P>The weight transfer is a big enough effect in a mustang that it gets better tracton in the rear then in the front.  But, it is small enough in a neon that it gets better traction in the front.<P>as for ice and snow... have you ever drivien a mustang 5.0 in the snow (new ones are better with trac control). I would take a neon for driving in the snow and a mustang for the dry street.  But I'd take a high powered fiero for both if I could only have one car!!||||reg||1
Z||000012||Oreif||09-04-2001||10:25 AM||||FWD is better in Snow/Rain when driving normal. Many improvements have cut back on torque steer and with traction control they work well.<BR>RWD is the preferred method for racing. For road courses a 50/50 wieght distribution is best. For drag racing weight is shifted to the rear wheels when launcing. Also for "durability" RWD is easier to maintain. Less things to break.<BR>Just go to any Taxi's or Police station's garage and ask them about it. Since GM stopped making the Caprice (RWD) they had to switch to Ford. The FWD versions offered by GM and Ford do not hold up well in a constant use/city driving environment. They require more maintanence more often. <P>With high HP, You can put on wider tires and wheelie bars to aid in getting traction on the launch. FWD will just spin.||||reg||1
Z||000013||btoth||09-04-2001||10:52 AM||||I was thinking along the same lines as far as the shift in weight goes.  When you accelerate in a RWD car, the weight gets shifted back which just ads to the force being applied down on the tires giving them better traction.  FWD would already have the max force pushing down on the tires while stopped, basically the weight of the engine. So, you would think that FWD would as far as straight line accerlation goes.  Does that make sense?  <P>For an example, I mentioned to my friend that if you took a Mustang GT auto, and a Grand Prix GTP auto... both pretty close in power (I think) and floored them both... which would go?  The GTP would just sit there and spin (from my expierences with them), the Mustang would just chirp it's tires and take off.  I'm sure there's some other factors involved too, like weight and stuff.<P>There has to be a way to <I>prove</I> one or the other.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||6
Z||000014||WampusCat||09-04-2001||11:14 AM||||Car and Driver did a comparison in an article on "best handling cars" a couple of years ago and decided that although some FWD cars handle very well, (Prelude was very highly rated)the front tires just get overwhelmed when trying to do the duty of steering, most of braking and accelerating.  The rear wheel cars just have an advantage by distributing the workload and having the weight transfering to the driven wheels when accelerating.  I think a lot of it depends on what the driver is most familiar with.  I know I prefer rear wheel drive cars and the only front driver that I owned,('84 Laser) I wrecked by trying to use RWD techniques to recover from oversteer.  The newer FWD cars are of course much better than that POS was, and my wife's Volvo S40 seems to handle pretty well, although I've never driven it "agressively."||||reg||1
Z||000015||Urchin||09-04-2001||11:49 AM||||I have to agree with Formula, Low powered cars I don't hink it makes much of a difference, but due to the weight transfer in a higher powered car, RWD would be better.  On the alternate discussion that has risen (snow and ice).   I've never understood why people claim FWD to be better in snow.  In a FWD vehicle you have practically no control.  You come to a stop sign, turn left, accelerate and slide helplessly into a snow bank if you over accelerate.  Or driving down the highway about to make a right turn, the wheels lock up and you have no control, and again you find yourself neck deep in snow.  RWD, if you have any driving skill you can bail yourself out of most situations.  Not in a FWD car.  As far as I am concerned FWD is better in the snow for people who don't know how to keep a rear wheel drive car under control.  Just my thoughts...<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/side_cut_sig_4.jpg">  <BR>If you can read this, I'm parked.||||reg||1
Z||000016||ray b||09-04-2001||11:59 AM||||the one and only succesfull frontdrive roadracer was the mini-cooper [low power lite] no others even try to race. like all nasty cars are front drive stock but conv to rear to race.at the drags fronts run in their own class as they have no chance against rear drive.<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000017||JohnnyK||09-04-2001||03:29 PM||||Urchin: Maybe you just don't know how to drive EITHER fwd or rwd?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> As I said before, no one will ADMIT that they like FWD better. But I drive both constantly in snow and ice, and the fwd is much better. A rwd is much to fidgity. I give it a bit of gas (it's a big van mind you) and the back end goes sliding around the front. It's not too fun. Half the people here haven't even driven in "snow and ice" though&gt; I'm not talking about 1 inch of snow here..||||reg||1
Z||000018||Mach10||09-04-2001||04:15 PM||||FWD is a LOT more forgiving in harsh conditions. If you gas it too much, the steering wheel jerks. Oh, well. On Rear-wheel drive, the back-end swings. Bye fender...<BR>Same thing around corners on ice. Touch the gas too hard, and you're in a 4-tire slide into the curb. The FWD just starts under-steering. A lot better to control, and a lot less freaky to the less-experienced driver.<P>RWD is a lot nicer to control on dry pavement, and is pretty predictable, but nothing (except AWD) will beat FWD in ice/snow. <P>NOW, Having stated all that, I don't beleive that either of them is better/worse AS LONG AS YOU KNOW HOW TO DRIVE SMART. <BR>Nothing will save you if you are going too fast around a corner on ice. The difference in drive-trains will only decide which fender gets creamed. Driver skill is the determining factor: How fast you can safely go, following distance, what NOT to do in an emergency etc.<BR>What drivertrain is best is determined by what the driver is comfortable with. I learned on FWD. I STILL have some bad habits that worked well with FWD, but are disasterous with RWD (DRopping the throttle when she oversteers... D-oh! But I think I've gotten control over that impulse). However, after 6 months, I am more comfortable with the predictability of RWD on dry pavement. We'll see how it goes after a Manitoba Winter (Beleive-you-me it deserves the caps)<P>||||reg||7
Z||000019||fieronv||09-04-2001||04:36 PM||||FWD do drive better on snow and icy conditions. I spent 3 year in Alaska testing equiptment for the Army and I test drove a variety of vehicle in the winter. With that said I still prefer RWD for every day driving becuase a RWD will "hook up" and corner better than FWD vehicles. Just a look at any race track you find that the winning cars are RWD, I will have to go with the majority of engineers of race cars who have spent millions of dollars developing the best and fastest cars. I still like Mid with RWD the best.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>85GT (twoloud)||||reg||12
Z||000020||Urchin||09-04-2001||04:38 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by JohnnyK:<BR><B>Urchin: Maybe you just don't know how to drive EITHER fwd or rwd?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> As I said before, no one will ADMIT that they like FWD better. But I drive both constantly in snow and ice, and the fwd is much better. A rwd is much to fidgity. I give it a bit of gas (it's a big van mind you) and the back end goes sliding around the front. It's not too fun. Half the people here haven't even driven in "snow and ice" though&gt; I'm not talking about 1 inch of snow here..</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Maybe I shouldn't have used those words  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  You are right though, I am not very good with FWD in the snow. I've ended up in the ditch twice  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">.  Never with a RWD.  Maybe that is because RWD is what I am acustom to. <BR>During the rain we had here the other day, I was at a traffic light.  The light turned green, I stepped on the gas (in my FWD Z24) and the tires slipped and the front end began to venture into the leftlane which was occupied by another car.  Naturally I let off of the gas.  Scared the hell out of me though (I'm not used to FWD).  It just seems that the front end will go wherever it feels like in slippery conditions. I have not experienced that in a RWD vehicle.    <P><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/side_cut_sig_4.jpg">  <BR>If you can read this, I'm parked.||||reg||1
Z||000021||Neal||09-04-2001||09:00 PM||||Urchin, ill agree with you 100% about FWD in harsh conditions.<P>My experience is that if you put on HIGH QUALITY snow/ice tires you will see the most benifit, much more than switching to FWD or AWD.  RWD is more forgiving if you do find yourself in a spin.  a plus because if you hit some nasty black ice it doesnt matter what your driving...<P>Now for the real shocker...  the best car ive ever driven in the winter  my FIERO with yoakhama guard X winter tires 185-70-r14's  It has proven itself FAR maore stable than any other car ive driven in the winter.||||reg||1
Z||000022||FieroKyle||09-04-2001||10:10 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Formula:<BR><B>lower powered cars..... fwd is better (neon, cavalier, etc..)<P>hig power cars...... rwd is better (mustang, camaro, etc...)<P>The weight transfer is a big enough effect in a mustang that it gets better tracton in the rear then in the front.  But, it is small enough in a neon that it gets better traction in the front.<P>as for ice and snow... have you ever drivien a mustang 5.0 in the snow (new ones are better with trac control). I would take a neon for driving in the snow and a mustang for the dry street.  But I'd take a high powered fiero for both if I could only have one car!!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I wouldn't say that, the Bonneville SSEI, Monte Carlo SS, and Grand Prix GTP are all FWD and can kick 85% to 95% cars on the road asses. But like everyone is saying, RWD is the best for racing and higher end performance overall. For a year round cruiser, FWD.||||reg||1
Z||000023||JasonMr2||09-04-2001||10:23 PM||||Ok, First of all the original question at the top didn’t mention anything about "harsh conditions"  and lets even leave handling out of it as well.<P>From a pure physics standpoint the question is......<P>Would a Fwd car accelerate as fast as a Rwd car given the same power??<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000024||Pontiaddict||09-04-2001||11:17 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by FieroKyle:<BR><B> I wouldn't say that, the Bonneville SSEI, Monte Carlo SS, and Grand Prix GTP are all FWD and can kick 85% to 95% cars on the road asses. But like everyone is saying, RWD is the best for racing and higher end performance overall. For a year round cruiser, FWD.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>That's becasue 85 to 95 percent of the cars on the road are either FWD, 5000lb suv's, or Ford Trucks with the insanely underpowered 4.6l in it.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Pontiaddict (edited 09-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000025||artherd||09-04-2001||11:20 PM||||Jeeeez, you guys aren't kidding!<P>Just get back into your Fiero, and trust that it's best...<P>wow.<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000026||Cooter||09-04-2001||11:34 PM||||The reason for so many front drive cars is because they are easier and cheaper to build. Not necessarily better or worse, but the almighty dollar decides what the platform will be. When the entire engine/driveline is installed as a unit, it saves a lot of assembly time and time is money on the line. The reason the BonneSSEI, MonteSS and GTP are front drive is not for performance- just ask the GM engineers what they really wanted to build- it is for ease of build and lower cost.||||reg||1
Z||000027||Brandon86SE||09-05-2001||01:56 AM||||I have to agree with Neal:  My Fiero is the best winter car I've ever driven, of course that might be because it's pretty much the only winter car I've ever driven.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Next to my mom's Geo Tracker of course.  (It's not my fault, I didn't have a car then)  But anyway, for harsh conditions (I've spent a winter in Calgary, and a winter in Medicine Hat with my Fiero) I'll admit it handles great!  Certainly, I can't drive like I do in the summer, but that makes spring time all that more exciting.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Having the weight right on the drive wheels helps a lot.  The only thing I've run into trouble with is parking lots with snow higher than the clearance under my car..... on the morning of a mid-term....  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  Oh well, I survived.  Oh, and that patch of black ice on Glenmore that sent me doing 360's down the road (accident free)<P>Brandon||||reg||1
Z||000028||JohnnyK||09-05-2001||02:10 AM||||Two things.. Front wheel drive is more expensive to build, but more consumers want FWD. <P>Physics, you can't ask that question, cause it depends on a whole lot of variables.||||reg||1
Z||000029||DRH||09-05-2001||02:18 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by JasonMr2:<BR><B>Ok, First of all the original question at the top didn’t mention anything about "harsh conditions"  and lets even leave handling out of it as well.<P>From a pure physics standpoint the question is......<P>Would a Fwd car accelerate as fast as a Rwd car given the same power??</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I think it depends on how much power. If it doesn't have enough to spin a tire it doesn't matter. Maybe FWD is better in a certain power range where it helps to have the weight over the tire because there is not enough power to shift much weight to the back. At some point RWD will be better. The torque of taking off will try to rotate the car around it's drive axle. In a FWD this means weight transfers to the back giving less traction in the front where it's needed. In a RWD it lifts the front and puts more weight on the back giving more traction.<P>I've never seen a FWD Top Fueler or Funny Car... I can't imagine they would even work right...||||reg||1
Z||000030||Cooter||09-05-2001||08:27 AM||||edited because I remembered that it is not worth arguing with someone on the internet. The special olympics picture comes to mind.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Cooter (edited 09-05-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000031||JohnnyK||09-05-2001||02:03 PM||||Heres one... "Whats better than winning a gold in the special olympics?"<P>"Not being Cooter!"  <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000032||btoth||09-05-2001||02:14 PM||||Why does everything end up being an argument?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"><P>The question was:  If you take two cars;  same body, same weight (not including the extra weight of the driveshaft for the RWD, we do know this is a factor); Same engine up front, same suspension, same size tires, etc. etc.  Which would be better, at least as far as acceleration (0-60 for example, or even 1/4 mi.)?  And we're talking dry pavement, no ice or whatever.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000033||ray b||09-05-2001||02:47 PM||||if you have ice and snow to drive in MOVE! or buy a jeep.<BR>fd is because its cheaper to build<BR>ever see a bmw or porsche with fd???<BR>when did anyone demand a all fd gm lineup???<BR>the bean counters did it not the car guys.<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000034||Neal||09-05-2001||02:52 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by btoth:<BR><B>The question was:  If you take two cars;  same body, same weight (not including the extra weight of the driveshaft for the RWD, we do know this is a factor); Same engine up front, same suspension, same size tires, etc. etc.  Which would be better, at least as far as acceleration (0-60 for example, or even 1/4 mi.)?  And we're talking dry pavement, no ice or whatever.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Given those specifics the RWD would win an acceleration contest, better initial hookup would give it the edge but i think after the initial hookup, once both cars have traction acceleration would be the SAME.||||reg||1
Z||000035||StuGood||09-05-2001||03:11 PM||||I'm glad you guys already covered the weight transfer thing, because you did it pretty well, IMO, and concisely.  'Fit were me, I'd still be on paragraph 2 of "simplifying assumptions."  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  So, for "overall racing" (a tractor pull?)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">, RWD has some significant advantages. <P>At the same time, in adverse weather conditions, I really like FWD.  But I'm not racing, of course.  I just want to get somewhere.  <B>Some</B> FWD cars might do well in certain rally-type races.   <P>What else comes to mind?  If I wanted to climb a slippery hill (and wanted to stay pointed uphill), I might prefer a rear-engined vehicle.  Going down the other side, I'd probably rather have the engine up front.  Maybe I need a car where the engine can slide from front to back as I desire.  That center hump would sure crowd things, though  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  <BR> ||||reg||6
Z||000036||Steve Normington||09-05-2001||03:51 PM||||I have a simple way to prove that RWD provides better acceleration.<P>Take your Fiero and race it down the quarter mile. The ET will be the time for the RWD car. Now race the same quarter mile in reverse with your car backwards on the track. Since the drive wheels are in the front (front regarding the direction of acceleration), the ET will be the time for a FWD car. Unless you have made some serious mods, the RWD will have a much better ET that the FWD. Now, no more of this bickering.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000037||Cooter||09-05-2001||03:55 PM||||You win.||||reg||1
Z||000038||Tigger||09-05-2001||04:01 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Steve Normington:<BR><B>I have a simple way to prove that RWD provides better acceleration.<P>Take your Fiero and race it down the quarter mile. The ET will be the time for the RWD car. Now race the same quarter mile in reverse with your car backwards on the track. Since the drive wheels are in the front (front regarding the direction of acceleration), the ET will be the time for a FWD car. Unless you have made some serious mods, the RWD will have a much better ET that the FWD. Now, no more of this bickering.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>So that's why FWD cars have 1 forward gear, and 3, 4, 5, maybe 6 gears for reverse.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_dizzy.gif"><P>Personally, I prefer RWD, and I'll leave it at that.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
